\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Mechanism and regime structure.}
The Symbolic Mirage (\S\ref{sec:mirage}) manifests through two degradation channels: the \emph{direct} channel limits voting diversity (Eff-$K < 2$), while the \emph{indirect} channel causes MAX-SAT to degenerate to vote counting (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}). The bias ratio gradient ($4.97\times$ Mistral $> 3.93\times$ Qwen $> 2.88\times$ LLaMA) aligns with regime boundaries: high bias ($4.24\times$ weighted) at intermediate SC produces SICA's only positive trends; low bias ($2.66\times$) at high SC yields the worst degradation ($-4.41$\,pp). Thinking-mode degeneration ($\kappa = 0.91$, Qwen3) confirms the trend continues with capability. For practitioners, $C_0$ combined with SC accuracy predicts when symbolic augmentation is unlikely to help. A methodological note: Z3 satisfiability is invariant to variable renaming (alpha-equivalence); the 56.4\% zero-variable-overlap rate between cross-answer constraint pairs (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}) reflects genuine semantic isolation, not a naming artifact.

\paragraph{Broader implications.}
The independence-accuracy trade-off instantiates a well-known tension in ensemble learning. \citet{krogh1994neural} decompose ensemble error into average individual error minus voter disagreement (the ``ambiguity'' term); \citet{brown2005diversity} formalize the principle that ensemble benefit requires both accuracy \emph{and} diversity. Our $K_{\text{eff}}$ framework provides an analogous decomposition for LLM traces: the Symbolic Mirage maximizes individual accuracy but collapses the ambiguity term, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} < 2$ despite nominal $K = 12$. CoT faithfulness compounds this: \citet{turpin2024language} show that chain-of-thought traces do not always reflect actual reasoning. If CoT is unfaithful to internal computation, then the mirage (Eq.~\ref{eq:mirage}) is an inherent property of the extraction process, not a correctable artifact---our process-level analysis, where cosine similarity ($0.69$--$0.79$) far exceeds answer-level $\kappa$ ($0.41$--$0.74$), corroborates this deeper homogeneity (\S\ref{sec:process-kappa}).

\paragraph{Conditions and open questions.}
The Symbolic Mirage provides a mechanistic explanation for why LLMs cannot reliably self-correct \citep{huang2024selfcorrect, stechly2025selfverification}: a $4.97\times$ bias ratio persists across fifteen remediation families. Three conditions may shift the frontier: (a)~70B+ models, though thinking-mode degeneration ($\kappa = 0.91$) suggests bias may intensify with scale; (b)~trained extractors decoupled from the reasoning model's distribution; and (c)~architecturally diverse verification (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

\paragraph{Scale and language.}
All models range from 7B to 32B parameters; whether the independence bottleneck persists at larger scales (70B+) or with reasoning-specialized models is untested. All datasets are English-language logical reasoning benchmarks with discrete answer spaces. We do not evaluate trained FOL extractors, external solvers with verified grounding, or multilingual settings. The oracle analysis covers two formal logic benchmarks with gold annotations (FOLIO, ProofWriter); extending to commonsense or open-domain reasoning requires constructing comparable gold constraint sets.

\paragraph{Statistical power.}
The per-label complementarity analysis of the cross-architecture NLI result (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}) involves small cell sizes (e.g., 42 \textsc{False} problems, 69 \textsc{Unknown} problems); per-label McNemar tests would be underpowered. The bias ratio metric ($4.97\times$) was initially computed on a single condition (Mistral-7B FOLIO, $n = 204$); cross-model replication (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}) confirms the pattern but is limited to FOLIO. Four-seed replication shows 2 of 4 seeds yielding null or negative results, and the intermediate-SC region is narrow (SC $\approx$ 50--55\%). The process-level similarity metric uses TF-IDF representations, which may underweight semantic similarity not captured by lexical overlap. Process-$\kappa$ provides aggregate-level characterization of reasoning homogeneity but does not discriminate per-instance (AUROC $\approx 0.5$).

\paragraph{Computational cost trade-off.}
SICA requires $4.5\times$ the wall-clock time of SC (81.4 vs.\ 17.7\,min for 204 questions at $K{=}12$), with constraint extraction accounting for 76\% of overhead (\S\ref{sec:exp-setup}). This $3.5\times$ additional cost yields no significant accuracy gain on any condition, making the method impractical in its current form. Alternative prompting strategies (few-shot, chain-of-thought extraction) might improve extraction quality, though the oracle gap suggests the bottleneck is structural. Additional limitations regarding power analysis and deduplication strategy are discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:limitations-extended}.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} All code, data, and experimental scripts will be released upon publication to facilitate replication of SICA, the oracle controls, and all remediation experiments.

